":"If a sequence has limit $L$, what does the definition require after some index $N$?","options":["Every term with $n\\ \\ge N$ is within $\\varepsilon$ of $L$","Only one term after $N$ is within $\\varepsilon$ of $L$","Only terms with $n \\lt N$ matter","The terms must equal $L$ exactly"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6cc5acfed761dc088ae13835"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_of_a_sequence_652bf652_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement conflicts with uniqueness of limits of sequences?","options":["A sequence is divergent","Converges to two distinct limits.","A convergent sequence is bounded.","The sequence $\\frac{1}{n}$ converges to $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5b807b74b0fe78dec9d5d87c"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_of_a_sequence_652bf652_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the definition use 'for every $n\\ \\ge N$' rather than 'for some $n\\ \\ge N$'?","options":["Convergence only checks one late term","Convergence only checks the first term.","Convergence requires $N = 1$ always","Convergence needs all late terms close."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1eb590ccf474f4554a90401c"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_of_a_sequence_652bf652_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A sequence has infinitely many terms close to $L$, but also infinitely many later terms far from $L$. Which issue prevents concluding convergence to $L$ from the definition?","options":["The terms are not eventually all close to $L$","The first term is not specified","The sequence must be a function of $x$","The limit must be $+\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8efe6acf068ddf9d6b863f65"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_of_a_sequence_652bf652_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $a_n = \\frac{1}{n}$ and $L = 0$, which inequality is the relevant one in the convergence definition?","options":["$\\ \\left|n-0\\right| \\lt \\varepsilon$ for all sufficiently large $n$","$\\ \\left|\\dfrac1n-N\\right| \\lt 0$","$\\ \\left|\\varepsilon-n\\right| \\lt L$","$\\ \\left|\\dfrac1n-0\\right| \\lt \\varepsilon$ for all sufficiently large $n$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"45360cbe5b47d035518e1010"},{"id":"expansion_pack_044_limit_of_a_sequence_add_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Must a convergent sequence be bounded?","options":["Yes; convergence implies boundedness","No; convergent sequences can be unbounded","Only if it converges to $0$","Only if it is increasing"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6cd64717577efac6cd231a32"},{"id":"expansion_pack_044_limit_of_a_sequence_add_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{2n+1}{n}$?","options":["$2$","$1$","$0$","It diverges"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5213c294a34752fb778dcf23"},{"id":"expansion_pack_044_limit_of_a_sequence_add_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which observation proves that $a_n=(-1)^n$ does not have a real limit?","options":["Two subsequences differ in limit.","The sequence is bounded.","The first term is negative.","Every term is nonzero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"533ac100af68c813659b52f3"},{"id":"expansion_pack_044_limit_of_a_sequence_add_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $a_n=3$ for all $n$, what is $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n$?","options":["$3$","$0$","$1$","Does not exist"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c708dbeb6a9cfa410c29292"},{"id":"expansion_pack_044_limit_of_a_sequence_add_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $a_n\\to3$ and $b_n\\to4$, what is $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}(a_n+b_n)$?","options":["$7$","$12$","$1$","$3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedb